Z||000014||HotFieroGT||03-06-2000||12:28 AM||||Hey guys,<P>Check out the motor trend magazine webpage, they are using an M-62 Eaton supercharger on a 60 degree V-6 (3.4 to be exact).  262 horseower and 289 FT LBS of torque.  Looking forward to seeing this car (the new GTO concept car, a Pontiac Grand Am) at an auto show to see where they put the supercharger.  Might work nicely on a 2.8 Fiero engine!||||reg||1
Z||000015||pollock||03-06-2000||04:55 PM||||I've looked into the supercharger thing a bit; largely inspired by that sc grand am in motor trend.  <P>There's a company called Magnuson Products(www.magnuson-products.com) that does a lot of production and R&D work for Eaton.  It looks to me like you could get an M62 to work on the fiero.  I see two possibilities.  <P>1.  Mount the charger on top of the upper intake.  Would probably require a custom adapter plate, but there might be enough real estate on top of the intake to mount it direct.  Block off the throttle body opening on the intake, and remount the throttle body on the back of the charger.  Magnuson offers custom-length nose drives and pulleys.  I believe it might be necessary to cut down the height of the Fiero thermostat housing.  A tensioner pulley would have to be crammed in somewhere.  Extensive decklid mods required; the nose drive would run right through one of the decklid's main structural members.  <P>2.  The other possibility is to mount it like the Grand Am one:  off to the side (toward the back of a fiero, sorta parallel to the valve cover).  Then you'd have to relocate the dog bone, get a real long nose drive, and probably hack into the right shock tower a bit.  Some creative plumbing would be needed to route air through the charger and into the intake.  <P>I'm sure that that canadian kit mounts the charger underneath the car, in place of the a/c compressor.  I guess that's ok if you live in canada, but it's too hot for that in Texas.<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000016||terryk||03-06-2000||05:17 PM||||Regarding an Eaton on the Fiero V6:<P>I looked at this and it is extraordinarily impractical. The biggest challenge is integrating the fuel rail into the adapter to the lower manifold.  If this was done, the M-62 would work.  Then you need a way to drive it. Somewhere I have a rough sketch of the adpater with the fuel rail built in. <P>You could switch to a TB with injectors and plug the lower plenum. I don't think the Eaton would object to having fuel sprayed through it. Might lead to lean cylinders though. That would solve the fuel rail issue and add others. The problem is the ECM. I don't know if the 6869 or 7170 will drive PH injectors (don't think so). The 7749 will if the jumpers are changed. <P>I have a couple M-62's and an M-90. I thought it would be a nice add-on. <P>I think a Paxton or Vortec mounted backwards where the battery is would be easier. The brackets would need to be mounted to the motor. The blower would need to be a reverse spin, but that's a minor detail. The oil plumbing problem comes back though.<P>Finally, I'm not giving up AC! You can have it when you peel my dead, frosty hands from the evaporator!<P>TK<P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 03-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||88 Fiero||03-06-2000||05:28 PM||||My girlfriend would not even allowed me get rid of my Fiero... She thinks I should finished my project...Anyway, as far as the supercharger.. RS Motorsports in Canada has a Supercharger kit for the Fiero. The only thing tho' is that you have to compromise your AC because that's where the supercharger is resciding where the AC compressor. But for me...it will be a 3.4 liter with a turbo intercooled. A guy in the club have a Auto 3.4 liter intercooled turbo Fiero with a custom headers on it and can leave a 6 foot skidmark on the pavement...that's wicked...<BR>||||reg||1
